    {
      "id": "lesson-2-4-strings-are-fixed-values",
      "number": "2.4",
      "title": "Strings are Fixed Values",
      "summary": "Remember the very first Python code you wrote?",
      "status": "available",
      "estimatedMinutes": 5,
      "tags": [
        "python-data-types"
      ],
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "page-2-4-paragraph-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": "Remember the very first Python code you wrote?",
          "format": "plain"
        },
        {
          "id": "page-2-4-table-2",
          "type": "table",
          "caption": "",
          "columns": [
            {
              "key": "column-1",
              "header": "Column 1",
              "align": "left"
            },
            {
              "key": "column-2",
              "header": "Column 2",
              "align": "left"
            }
          ],
          "rows": [
            {
              "column-1": "print(\"welcome\")"
            },
            {
              "column-1": "Run Code >>",
              "column-2": "Run Code >>"
            },
            {
              "column-1": "Run Code >>"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "page-2-4-paragraph-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": "We asked you to type it exactly like that — and here's why.",
          "format": "plain"
        },
        {
          "id": "page-2-4-paragraph-4",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": "In Python, strings are case sensitive. That means uppercase and lowercase letters are treated as completely different.",
          "format": "plain"
        },
        {
          "id": "page-2-4-paragraph-5",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": "For example:",
          "format": "plain"
        },
        {
          "id": "page-2-4-paragraph-6",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": "- \"welcome\" is not the same as \"WELCOME\"\n- \"welcome\" is not the same as \"Welcome\"\n- \"welcome\" is not the same as \"WelCome\"",
          "format": "markdown"
        },
        {
          "id": "page-2-4-paragraph-7",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": "The letters have to match exactly — same spelling, same case.",
          "format": "plain"
        },
        {
          "id": "page-2-4-paragraph-8",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": "Similarly, spaces matter too.",
          "format": "plain"
        },
        {
          "id": "page-2-4-paragraph-9",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": "Even a small space can change the string completely.",
          "format": "plain"
        },
        {
          "id": "page-2-4-paragraph-10",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": "- \"welcome\" is not the same as \"wel come\" (there's a space in the middle)\n- \"welcome\" is not the same as \" welcome\" (space at the beginning)\n- \"welcome\" is not the same as \"welcome \" (space at the end)",
          "format": "markdown"
        },
        {
          "id": "page-2-4-paragraph-11",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": "To us, it might look similar — but Python sees it as something totally different.",
          "format": "plain"
        },
        {
          "id": "page-2-4-paragraph-12",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": "So always double-check your spelling, your casing, and your spacing.",
          "format": "plain"
        },
        {
          "id": "page-2-4-paragraph-13",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": "Next, let's learn about numbers in Python.",
          "format": "plain"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "lesson-2-5-numbers",
      "number": "2.5",
      "title": "Numbers",
      "summary": "The two common types of numbers in Python are:",
      "status": "available",
      "estimatedMinutes": 5,
      "tags": [
        "python-data-types"
      ],
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "page-2-5-paragraph-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": "The two common types of numbers in Python are:",
          "format": "plain"
        },
        {
          "id": "page-2-5-heading-2",
          "type": "heading",
          "level": 3,
          "text": "1. Integers"
        },
        {
          "id": "page-2-5-paragraph-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": "Integers are numbers without decimal points. They can be positive, negative, or zero. Examples include 5, -11, 0 and 12.",
          "format": "plain"
        },
        {
          "id": "page-2-5-heading-4",
          "type": "heading",
          "level": 3,
          "text": "2. Floating-Point Numbers"
        },
        {
          "id": "page-2-5-paragraph-5",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": "Floating-point numbers contain decimal points. Like integers, they can also be positive, negative, or zero. Examples include 2.5, 6.76, 0.0, and -9.45.",
          "format": "plain"
        },
        {
          "id": "page-2-5-paragraph-6",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": "You'll learn how to print these numbers—but first, take a quick quiz on floating-point numbers.",
          "format": "plain"
        }
      ]
    },
